#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228891 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb295d6c-62fe-59bc-a139-49785ac98f1f" :knowledge-point/title "Using Linearity of Fourier Series"}] :question/id #uuid "cf8f2367-68af-5ef4-ac2e-5a73e37695d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-21803" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx) \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{{c}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{c}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{d}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2\\pi$-periodic extensions of some functions from $x \\in [-\\pi,\\pi)$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with the Fourier series shown above.  What are the Fourier constant coefficient, the $3\\textrm{rd}$ Fourier sine coefficient, and the $8\\textrm{th}$ Fourier cosine coefficient of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$?" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2\\pi$-periodic functions with Fourier series\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)), \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{{c}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({c}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{d}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen for any constants $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$,\n\n$$\n\\alpha f(x) + \\beta g(x)\n\\sim\n\\frac{\\alpha a_0 + \\beta c_0}{2}\n+\n\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\n\\Big(\n(\\alpha a_n + \\beta c_n)\\cos(nx)\n+\n(\\alpha b_n + \\beta d_n)\\sin(nx)\n\\Big).\n$$\n\nUsing the linearity property of Fourier series, the corresponding Fourier coefficients of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$ are\n\n- $-3a_0+4c_0$ for the constant coefficient,\n\n- $-3a_n+4c_n,$ $n \\ge 1$ for the cosine coefficients, and\n\n- $-3b_n+4d_n,$ $n \\ge 1$ for the sine coefficients.\n\nTherefore,\n\n- the Fourier constant coefficient of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$ is $-3a_0+4c_0$,\n\n- the $3$rd Fourier sine coefficient of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$ is $-3b_3+4d_3$, and\n\n- the $8$th Fourier cosine coefficient of $-3f(x)+4g(x)$ is $-3a_8+4c_8$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228892 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "532a73e6-e026-57c9-9bad-57cd2f75b6df" :knowledge-point/title "Parity Recognition and Series Type"}] :question/id #uuid "f43f5028-9314-5123-bbd8-85dc5c4a9b2b" :question/math-academy-id "e-21804" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=x\\cos(2x)$ on $x \\in [-\\pi,\\pi].$ Complete the statements below by filling in the blanks.\nDetermine if the function $f(x)$ is odd or even, and which terms of the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero." :question/worked-solution "To determine whether $f$ is even or odd, we compare $f(-x)$ and $f(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(-x)) \\\\  & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2x) \\\\  & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x) = -f(x),$ we have that $f$ is odd.\n\nFor Fourier series on $[-\\pi,\\pi],$ the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\na_n=\\frac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\cos(nx)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand the constant coefficient is\n\n$$\na_0=\\frac{1}{\\pi}\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi} f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is odd and $\\cos(nx)$ is even, so $f(x)\\cos(nx)$ is odd. Also, $f(x)$ itself is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi,\\pi]$ is $0.$ Thus, $a_n=0$ for all $n\\ge1,$ and $a_0=0.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{odd}}$ and the $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{cosine}}$ terms  in its Fourier series must be zero."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228893 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f30ca427-3074-56c3-828a-1327d7b172b0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Effects of Vertical Shifting and Horizontal Reflection on Coefficients"}] :question/id #uuid "848cc0af-b940-5ead-81ac-49cc6d2f4989" :question/math-academy-id "e-21805" :question/problem 