atorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=-f(x),$ we have that $f$ is odd.\n\nFor Fourier series on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand the constant coefficient is\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is odd and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)$ is even, so $f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)$ is odd. Also, $f(x)$ itself is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is $0.$ Thus, ${a}_{n}=0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{0}=0.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\text{odd}$ and the $\\text{cosine terms}$ in its Fourier series must be zero."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354636 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb295d6c-62fe-59bc-a139-49785ac98f1f" :knowledge-point/title "Using Linearity of Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "acd90811-950c-5e89-b9ac-ae453190dd3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{a}_{5}-2{b}_{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354638} {:answer/id #uuid "e8044555-6bfb-5838-a9d3-da76fb245a5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{c}_{0}-2{d}_{0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354639} {:answer/id #uuid "f42d62f7-8467-5215-a939-0cdc4f7f1dd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{b}_{5}-2{d}_{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354640} {:answer/id #uuid "c4142b8a-90dc-5fc5-a4df-2b8f59504978" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{a}_{5}-2{c}_{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354641} {:answer/id #uuid "f99ee61e-8975-5bba-8302-cab0c924884e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{a}_{0}-2{d}_{0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4142b8a-90dc-5fc5-a4df-2b8f59504978" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{a}_{5}-2{c}_{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354641} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c26ae33-f299-56ce-9294-1eeb93b617d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354637}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf2b9cd4-9ea6-5ccf-a551-cb0203699d02" :question/math-academy-id "q-340756" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx) \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{{c}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{c}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{d}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2\\pi $-periodic extensions of some functions from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R$ with the Fourier series shown above. What is the $5\\text{th}$ Fourier cosine coefficient of $-3f(x)-2g(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ be $2\\pi $-periodic functions with Fourier series\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)), \\\\ g(x) & ∼\\frac{{c}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({c}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{d}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen for any constants $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $,\n\n$$\n\\alpha f(x)+\\beta g(x)∼\\frac{\\alpha {a}_{0}+\\beta {c}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }((\\alpha {a}_{n}+\\beta {c}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+(\\alpha {b}_{n}+\\beta {d}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nUsing the linearity property of Fourier series, the corresponding Fourier coefficients of $-3f(x)-2g(x)$ are\n\n- $-3{a}_{0}-2{c}_{0}$ for the constant coefficient,\n\n- $-3{a}_{n}-2{c}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1$ for the cosine coefficients, and\n\n- $-3{b}_{n}-2{d}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1$ for the sine coefficients.\n\nTherefore, the $5$th cosine coefficient of $-3f(x)-2g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n-3{a}_{5}-2{c}_{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 1